cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Open Sustainable Finance Data: a catalogue of the datasets behind 109 empirical sustainable finance papers"
message: "If you use this catalogue, please cite it using these metadata."
type: dataset
authors:
  - given-names: Bastien
    family-names: Tognolini
    email: bastien.tognolini@uzh.ch
    affiliation: "University of Zurich, Department of Banking and Finance"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-08-21
license: CC-BY-4.0
license-url: "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"
keywords:
  - sustainable finance
  - open data
  - reproducibility
  - replication
  - research data management
  - ESG
  - climate finance
  - biodiversity finance
  - systematic literature review
abstract: >-
  A catalogue of the datasets used by 109 empirical sustainable finance papers
  published in the Journal of Finance, Journal of Financial Economics, Review of
  Financial Studies and Review of Finance through April 2026. Each paper is
  hand-coded on topic, method, raw data source, temporal coverage, geographic
  scope of the data and unit of observation, and scored on two openness
  dimensions — data availability and replication code availability. Unlike a
  conventional open-data index the corpus is not conditioned on openness:
  papers releasing nothing are included, so that the openness rate of the field
  is measurable rather than assumed. Access links carry recorded verification
  verdicts and a check date.

# --- Optional: replace the placeholders below once the repository is public ---
#
# repository-code: "https://github.com/<user>/open-sustainable-finance-data"
# url: "https://github.com/<user>/open-sustainable-finance-data"
#
# --- Minting a DOI (about five minutes, and worth doing before you cite it) ---
#
# 1. Push this repository to GitHub as a public repository.
# 2. Sign in at https://zenodo.org with the GitHub account, then open
#    Account -> GitHub and flip the switch next to this repository ON.
# 3. On GitHub, create a release tagged v1.0.0. Zenodo archives the tag and
#    mints a DOI within a minute or two.
# 4. Zenodo issues two DOIs: a *concept* DOI that always resolves to the newest
#    version, and a *version* DOI fixed to v1.0.0. Cite the version DOI in the
#    thesis — a reader following it must land on the numbers the thesis reports.
# 5. Add both back here:
#
#      doi: 10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX          # version DOI for v1.0.0
#      identifiers:
#        - type: doi
#          value: 10.5281/zenodo.YYYYYYY    # concept DOI, all versions
#          description: "Concept DOI resolving to the latest version"
#
# 6. Re-run `make build` and commit. GitHub renders a "Cite this repository"
#    button from this file.
